          "title": "What Belady's anomaly really means",
          "text": "Intuition says more memory can only help. Belady and his colleagues showed in 1969 that this intuition is false for FIFO. With more frames, FIFO keeps a different — and sometimes worse — set of pages, because the eviction order depends only on load order, not on usefulness. It is not a bug in the arithmetic; it is a genuine property of FIFO. Mention the year and the name in your answer for full marks."
        },
        {
          "title": "The stack property, in plain words",
          "text": "In LRU, the pages held in 3 frames are always the 3 most recently used pages, and the pages held in 4 frames are always the 4 most recently used. The first set is automatically <em>inside</em> the second. So anything that was a hit with 3 frames must also be a hit with 4 frames, and faults can only go down. FIFO has no such nesting — the 3-frame set can contain a page that the 4-frame run has already thrown away."
        },
