      "worked": "<strong>Part A — FIFO with 3 frames</strong><br>Reference string: <code>7, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 0, 4, 2, 3, 0, 3, 2</code> (13 references)<br>Frames shown oldest → newest.<br><br><code>7</code> → FAULT. Frames: [7]<br><code>0</code> → FAULT. Frames: [7, 0]<br><code>1</code> → FAULT. Frames: [7, 0, 1]<br><code>2</code> → FAULT, evict 7 (oldest). Frames: [0, 1, 2]<br><code>0</code> → <strong>HIT</strong>. Frames: [0, 1, 2]<br><code>3</code> → FAULT, evict 0. Frames: [1, 2, 3]<br><code>0</code> → FAULT, evict 1. Frames: [2, 3, 0]<br><code>4</code> → FAULT, evict 2. Frames: [3, 0, 4]<br><code>2</code> → FAULT, evict 3. Frames: [0, 4, 2]<br><code>3</code> → FAULT, evict 0. Frames: [4, 2, 3]<br><code>0</code> → FAULT, evict 4. Frames: [2, 3, 0]<br><code>3</code> → <strong>HIT</strong>. Frames: [2, 3, 0]<br><code>2</code> → <strong>HIT</strong>. Frames: [2, 3, 0]<br><br><strong>Total page faults = 10</strong> · Hits = 3 · Hit ratio = 3/13 ≈ 0.23<br>(Notice 0 was evicted at step 6 and needed again immediately at step 7 — FIFO's classic mistake.)<br><br><strong>Part B — Belady's anomaly, proved with numbers</strong><br>New reference string: <code>1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</code> (12 references)<br><br><em>FIFO with <strong>3 frames</strong>:</em><br><code>1</code> F [1] · <code>2</code> F [1,2] · <code>3</code> F [1,2,3]<br><code>4</code> F evict 1 → [2,3,4]<br><code>1</code> F evict 2 → [3,4,1]<br><code>2</code> F evict 3 → [4,1,2]<br><code>5</code> F evict 4 → [1,2,5]<br><code>1</code> <strong>HIT</strong> · <code>2</code> <strong>HIT</strong><br><code>3</code> F evict 1 → [2,5,3]<br><code>4</code> F evict 2 → [5,3,4]<br><code>5</code> <strong>HIT</strong><br>Hits = 3 → <strong>Page faults = 12 − 3 = 9</strong><br><br><em>FIFO with <strong>4 frames</strong>:</em><br><code>1</code> F [1] · <code>2</code> F [1,2] · <code>3</code> F [1,2,3] · <code>4</code> F [1,2,3,4]<br><code>1</code> <strong>HIT</strong> · <code>2</code> <strong>HIT</strong><br><code>5</code> F evict 1 → [2,3,4,5]<br><code>1</code> F evict 2 → [3,4,5,1]<br><code>2</code> F evict 3 → [4,5,1,2]<br><code>3</code> F evict 4 → [5,1,2,3]<br><code>4</code> F evict 5 → [1,2,3,4]<br><code>5</code> F evict 1 → [2,3,4,5]<br>Hits = 2 → <strong>Page faults = 12 − 2 = 10</strong><br><br><strong>3 frames → 9 faults. 4 frames → 10 faults.</strong><br>We gave the system MORE memory and it performed WORSE. This is <strong>Belady's anomaly</strong>.<br><br><em>Compare with LRU on the very same string:</em> 3 frames gives 10 faults and 4 frames gives 8 faults — it goes down, as it must, because LRU is a stack algorithm.",
      "mistake": "Two errors to avoid. (1) Forgetting that the initial loads into empty frames are also page faults — with 3 frames, the first three distinct references are always 3 faults. (2) Treating FIFO like LRU: a HIT in FIFO does NOT change the queue order. The page stays exactly where it was in the queue.",
      "examAnswer": "The First In First Out page replacement algorithm selects for replacement the page that has been resident in memory for the longest time, irrespective of how frequently or how recently it has been used, and is implemented by maintaining the resident pages in a simple queue. Although it is easy to implement, it may replace a heavily used page and it exhibits Belady's anomaly, first reported in 1969, in which increasing the number of allocated frames causes the number of page faults to increase rather than decrease. For the reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, FIFO incurs nine page faults with three frames but ten page faults with four frames. Algorithms possessing the stack property, in which the set of pages resident in n frames is always a subset of the set resident in n + 1 frames, such as LRU and the Optimal algorithm, are provably free from this anomaly.",
