      "examAnswer": "The Least Recently Used algorithm replaces the resident page whose most recent reference lies furthest in the past, on the assumption, justified by the principle of locality of reference, that a page used recently is likely to be used again shortly. The Optimal algorithm replaces the page whose next reference lies furthest in the future and therefore produces the minimum possible number of page faults; however, since it requires knowledge of the future reference string it cannot be implemented and serves only as a standard against which practical algorithms are measured. The Most Recently Used algorithm replaces the page referenced most recently and is beneficial only for cyclic sequential access patterns such as repeated scans of a large file. Both LRU and the Optimal algorithm possess the stack property and are therefore free from Belady's anomaly.",
