    {
      "title": "Clock (Second Chance) Page Replacement",
      "teacher": "True LRU needs the hardware to update a timestamp on every single memory reference — far too expensive. So engineers asked: can we get most of LRU's benefit with just ONE extra bit per page? The answer is the Clock algorithm, and it is what real operating systems actually use.",
      "explain": "The <strong>Clock algorithm</strong>, also called <strong>Second Chance</strong>, is a cheap approximation of LRU. Each page has a single <strong>reference bit (R)</strong>, set to 1 by the hardware whenever the page is used. The resident pages are arranged in a circle with a pointer (the 'clock hand'). On a fault the hand examines the page it points to: if R = 1 it clears the bit to 0 and moves on (a second chance); if R = 0 that page is replaced.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "Needs only <strong>one reference bit per page</strong>, set automatically by hardware on every access.",
        "Resident pages form a <strong>circular list</strong>; a pointer (clock hand) sweeps around it.",
        "On a fault: R = 1 → set R = 0 and advance (second chance). R = 0 → <strong>replace this page</strong> and advance the hand past it.",
        "A newly loaded page gets R = 1; on a <strong>hit</strong>, the page's R is set to 1 and the hand does not move.",
        "If every page has R = 1, the hand goes all the way round clearing bits and then replaces the page it started at — so it <strong>degenerates to FIFO</strong>.",
        "It is a genuine <strong>approximation of LRU</strong>: a page used recently has R = 1 and survives at least one sweep.",
        "The <strong>enhanced second-chance</strong> version uses two bits, reference (R) and modify/dirty (M), and prefers to evict a page that is neither used nor modified, avoiding a disk write.",
        "Clock is what real systems use — full LRU is too expensive in hardware."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "Why it is called Second Chance",
          "text": "A page with R = 1 is not evicted immediately. Instead its bit is cleared and the hand moves on, so the page gets one more full sweep to prove it is still in use. If the program touches it again before the hand returns, R becomes 1 again and it is saved once more. If not, R is still 0 on the next visit and it is evicted. Only genuinely idle pages get thrown out."
        },
        {
          "title": "Enhanced second chance — two bits",
          "text": "Writing a modified (dirty) page back to disk costs an extra disk operation, so it is better to evict a clean page. Enhanced second chance classifies pages into four groups using (R, M): (0,0) not used and clean — the best victim; (0,1) not used but modified; (1,0) used and clean; (1,1) used and modified — the worst victim. The hand sweeps looking for the lowest class available, clearing R bits as it goes."
