      "worked": "<strong>Clock algorithm with 3 frames</strong><br>Reference string: <code>1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</code><br>Frames are slots f0, f1, f2 in a circle. R-bit shown in brackets. Hand starts at f0.<br><br><code>1</code> FAULT → f0 = 1(1). Hand → f1.  Frames: 1(1), —, —<br><code>2</code> FAULT → f1 = 2(1). Hand → f2.  Frames: 1(1), 2(1), —<br><code>3</code> FAULT → f2 = 3(1). Hand → f0.  Frames: 1(1), 2(1), 3(1)<br><code>4</code> FAULT. Hand at f0: page 1 has R=1 → clear to 0, move to f1. Page 2 R=1 → clear, move to f2. Page 3 R=1 → clear, move to f0. Page 1 now R=0 → <strong>replace</strong>. f0 = 4(1), hand → f1.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Frames: 4(1), 2(0), 3(0)<br><code>1</code> FAULT. Hand at f1: page 2 R=0 → <strong>replace</strong>. f1 = 1(1), hand → f2.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Frames: 4(1), 1(1), 3(0)<br><code>2</code> FAULT. Hand at f2: page 3 R=0 → <strong>replace</strong>. f2 = 2(1), hand → f0.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Frames: 4(1), 1(1), 2(1)<br><code>5</code> FAULT. Hand at f0: 4 R=1 → clear, f1. 1 R=1 → clear, f2. 2 R=1 → clear, f0. 4 now R=0 → <strong>replace</strong>. f0 = 5(1), hand → f1.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Frames: 5(1), 1(0), 2(0)<br><code>1</code> <strong>HIT</strong> → set page 1's R to 1. Hand stays at f1.  Frames: 5(1), 1(1), 2(0)<br><code>2</code> <strong>HIT</strong> → set page 2's R to 1.  Frames: 5(1), 1(1), 2(1)<br><code>3</code> FAULT. Hand at f1: 1 R=1 → clear, f2. 2 R=1 → clear, f0. 5 R=1 → clear, f1. 1 now R=0 → <strong>replace</strong>. f1 = 3(1), hand → f2.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Frames: 5(0), 3(1), 2(0)<br><code>4</code> FAULT. Hand at f2: page 2 R=0 → <strong>replace</strong>. f2 = 4(1), hand → f0.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Frames: 5(0), 3(1), 4(1)<br><code>5</code> <strong>HIT</strong> → set page 5's R to 1.  Frames: 5(1), 3(1), 4(1)<br><br><strong>Total page faults = 9</strong> · Hits = 3<br><br>On this string Clock matches FIFO's 9 faults, and it beats LRU's 10 — with only one extra bit of hardware. That cheapness is why real operating systems choose it.",
      "mistake": "Do not evict the first page the hand points at without checking its R bit. The hand may have to go round several positions — or even a full circle — clearing bits before it finds an R = 0 page. Also remember that a HIT only sets R = 1; it does not move the hand.",
      "examAnswer": "The Clock, or second chance, page replacement algorithm is a practical approximation of the Least Recently Used algorithm requiring only a single reference bit per page, which the hardware sets whenever the page is referenced. The resident pages are maintained in a circular list traversed by a pointer. When a page fault occurs, the pointer examines the page to which it refers: if the reference bit is one, the bit is cleared and the pointer advances, thereby granting that page a second chance; if the reference bit is zero, the page is selected as the victim and the pointer advances past it. If all reference bits are set, the algorithm degenerates into First In First Out. The enhanced second-chance variant additionally examines the modify bit so as to prefer a page that is both unreferenced and unmodified, thereby avoiding an unnecessary write to the backing store.",
