    {
      "title": "Segmentation, Segmented Paging and Paged Segmentation",
      "teacher": "Paging is brilliant for the hardware but stupid about meaning — it might cut your function in half across two pages. A programmer does not think in 4 KB blocks; she thinks 'this is the code, this is the stack, this is the array'. Segmentation is memory divided the way a programmer thinks.",
      "explain": "<strong>Segmentation</strong> divides a program into logical units of <strong>variable size</strong> called segments — code, data, stack, heap, a large array — each with its own name and length. A logical address is a pair <strong>&lt;segment number, offset&gt;</strong>. A <strong>segment table</strong> holds a <strong>base</strong> (where the segment starts in physical memory) and a <strong>limit</strong> (its length) for each segment.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "A <strong>segment</strong> is a variable-size logical unit: code, data, stack, heap, a function or an array.",
        "Logical address = <strong>&lt;segment number s, offset d&gt;</strong> — a <em>two-dimensional</em> address the programmer can see.",
        "Each segment-table entry has a <strong>base</strong> and a <strong>limit</strong>.",
        "Translation: if <strong>d &lt; limit</strong>, physical address = base + d. If <strong>d ≥ limit</strong>, a <strong>trap</strong> occurs (segmentation fault).",
        "Segmentation matches the programmer's view and makes <strong>protection and sharing per logical unit</strong> easy (e.g. mark the code segment read-only, share one library segment).",
        "Segmentation causes <strong>external fragmentation</strong>, because segments have different sizes.",
        "Paging causes internal fragmentation and is invisible to the programmer; segmentation causes external fragmentation and is visible.",
        "<strong>Segmented paging</strong> (also called paged segmentation): each segment is itself divided into pages — this removes external fragmentation while keeping the logical structure.",
        "The address then has <strong>three parts</strong>: segment number, page number within the segment, and offset within the page."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "Why segmentation exists at all",
          "text": "Paging splits memory by <em>size</em>, which has no relation to what the program means. A single 4 KB page might hold the end of one function and the start of another, so you cannot mark it read-only or share it sensibly. Segments follow the program's own structure, so the OS can give the code segment read-and-execute permission, the data segment read-and-write permission, and let ten processes share one copy of a library segment. That is protection and sharing done properly."
        },
        {
          "title": "Segmented paging — the best of both",
          "text": "The obvious combination: keep the logical division into segments, but store each segment as a set of pages instead of one contiguous block. Now segments no longer need contiguous physical memory, so <strong>external fragmentation disappears</strong>, while protection and sharing still work per segment. The price is an extra level of lookup: the CPU consults the segment table to find that segment's page table, then that page table to find the frame. A TLB hides most of this cost. The Intel x86 architecture used exactly this scheme."
        },
        {
          "title": "The names, and why they confuse everyone",
          "text": "Many textbooks use <strong>segmented paging</strong> and <strong>paged segmentation</strong> as two names for the same scheme, and most RGPV answers treat them as identical: segmentation on the outside, paging on the inside. Where a distinction is drawn, <em>paged segmentation</em> emphasises that each segment is paged (segment table → page table → frame), while <em>segmented paging</em> is sometimes used for a paged system in which the large page table is itself broken into segments. Write the standard two-level scheme, state clearly that both terms usually describe segments divided into pages, and you will be safe."
        }
