      "examAnswer": "Segmentation is a memory management scheme that supports the programmer's view of memory by dividing a program into logical units of variable length, such as code, data, stack and heap, each of which forms a segment. A logical address consists of a segment number and an offset; the segment number indexes a segment table whose entries contain a base and a limit, and the physical address is obtained as base plus offset only if the offset is strictly less than the limit, a violation causing a trap to the operating system. Segmentation provides natural protection and sharing at the level of logical units but suffers from external fragmentation because segments are of unequal size. Segmented paging, also referred to as paged segmentation, combines the two schemes by dividing each segment into fixed-size pages, so that external fragmentation is eliminated while the logical structure of segmentation is preserved, at the cost of an additional level of address translation.",
